# Agent memory, third batch: the cases that take memory_write to 100 (50 cases).
#
# Only the new cases live here; the first 16 stay in `memory.jsonl` and the next 34 in
# `memory-b.jsonl`, so both published runs stay reproducible exactly as they were.
#
# WHY A THIRD BATCH. `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty` measured this point at 26/34 under the
# shipped policy and 31/34 at a plain 0.5 cut, with AUC 0.99 and Cohen's kappa 1.00 between
# the two annotators: not one of its cases is disputed, so every one of those eight losses is
# the 0.70 threshold declining to act on a case nobody argues about. `benchmarks/thresholds.py`
# then refused to move the threshold, and was right to: with a 95 % Wilson lower bound the
# sample supported no target at all at n=34. The arithmetic it published is the reason this
# file exists - a 90 % precision target needs 35 ACTED cases, which at a balanced label mix
# is about 70 labelled ones. With these 50, memory_write has 100 cases and 51 `store` labels,
# which is the first time any point in this repository can carry a target worth having.
#
# THE DISTINCTION, unchanged from the second batch because changing it would invalidate it:
#
#   store: something the agent learned by doing, that it cannot get back by re-reading a
#          source it already holds. Operational constraints, the reason behind a decision,
#          a negative result and why, a standing instruction from the client.
#   skip:  anything re-derivable. A literal quotation from a document already in the volume,
#          a fact about code the agent can grep, restatements of the task, progress
#          narration, transient state, and general world knowledge.
#
# THE TRAP PAIRS, written so that surface form cannot decide the answer. Each pair is two
# cases that read alike and label opposite, which is the only way to tell a point that
# understands the criterion from one that has learned a register:
#
#   mw-69 / mw-85   a price the client authorised us to spend, against the same price
#                   published on the registry's own website.
#   mw-53 / mw-91   a negative result carrying the reason it failed, against a bare
#                   statement that something returned nothing.
#   mw-70 / mw-96   an opening constraint discovered by asking, against an address anyone
#                   can look up.
#   mw-60 / mw-97   a discrepancy we verified between two documents, against a name read
#                   off one document we already hold.
#   mw-63 / mw-80   a standing quota on an account, against the size of a file on disk.
#
# Annotator 1 (the author) labelled every case before any decision was run. Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model given the same question from the same builder, blind to the first
# label and to the evaluator's answer, run by `benchmarks/annotate.py`. Two annotators, one of
# them a model, is not two independent humans, and the paper says so wherever it uses these.
